    "en":  {
               "headline":  "QCOM breaks below $151 support as handset slump and chip rout collide",
               "technical":  "Trend is down. QCOM closed at $149.94 on 2026-11-20, about 13% below all three EMAs (exponential moving averages, which track the average price): EMA50 $172.71, EMA100 $174.42, EMA200 $172.77. The three lines are bunched together, and EMA50 has slipped just under EMA200. That points to a stalled trend that is turning down. Momentum is negative. MACD (the gap between the 12-day and 26-day EMAs) is -4.31, below its signal line at -2.72, and the histogram is -1.59. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 35.1: weak, but not yet oversold. The close was below the lower Bollinger Band at $151.28. The bands mark the normal range around the 20-day average, so a close below the lower band shows strong selling, though such moves often snap back partly. The day opened at $161.32 and closed near its $149.13 low, a 7.45% drop. Volume was 22.2M shares, about 1.5x the 20-day average, which confirms the selling. The price sits about 20% of the way up its 52-week range ($121.99–$259.92) and is 42% below the high. Resistance is around $161–162 and then the $170–175 EMA cluster. There is no clear support until the $122 52-week low.",
               "fundamental":  "On 2026-11-04 Qualcomm reported fiscal Q4 2026. Non-GAAP revenue was $10.44B (down 7% year on year) and EPS was $2.17, both slightly above forecasts. Handset revenue fell 26% to $5.14B. Apple is moving to its own modems, and high memory prices are hurting phone demand. Automotive revenue rose 89% to a record $1.99B. Licensing revenue (QTL, the patent business) was $1.30B, down 7%. The chip unit\u0027s pretax margin fell to 24% from 29%. Q1 FY27 guidance is revenue of $10.4–11.2B and non-GAAP EPS of $2.15–2.35, with data-center revenue above $650M. FY27 guidance assumes no Apple chip sales at all. For the AI value chain, Qualcomm is trying to shift from phones toward cars, PCs and AI inference in data centers. In September it signed a deal letting Amazon buy up to $60B of its data-center AI chips. The licensing moat is still in place but shrinking slowly.",
               "news":  [
                            "2026-11-04: Q4 FY26 results came in slightly ahead of estimates, but the profit outlook was weak and the chip margin was squeezed, so shares fell about 9.5% on 2026-11-05. CEO Cristiano Amon said revenue from outside handsets should \u0027replace Apple entirely\u0027.",
                            "At the 2026-11-04 call, management set a December-quarter data-center revenue target above $650M, ahead of the roughly $539M Wall Street expected. It repeated its goal of about $5B in data-center revenue for the year.",
                            "2026-11-09: Freedom Capital Markets cut QCOM to Hold from Buy and lowered its target to $185 from $200. Morgan Stanley also cut its target to $197 from $220 and kept Equal Weight.",
                            "2026-11-18: Qualcomm and Cirrascale launched a cloud service on the Dragonfly AI200 inference platform, so customers can test their own AI workloads before buying.",
                            "2026-11-20: A broad chip selloff, led by AMD and Micron, pulled the Nasdaq down sharply on worries about regulation, valuations and interest rates. QCOM fell 7.45% that day, and we found no news specific to Qualcomm."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The technical picture is clearly negative. The price is below every EMA, MACD is falling and volume confirms the selling. The fundamentals also have real weak spots. Handset revenue, the core business, fell 26%. Apple chip revenue is expected to drop to zero. The chip margin fell 5 points, and capital spending more than doubled to $2.49B, which cut free cash flow. The data-center story has not yet delivered material revenue. If the sector-wide selloff deepens, the $122 52-week low is the next clear support.",
               "opportunity":  "The stock is cheap and some parts of the business are growing fast. The trailing P/E is about 11.8 and the dividend yield about 2.45%. FY26 buybacks plus dividends totalled $11.7B. Automotive revenue rose 89%, and the data-center target beat expectations. Much of the Nov 20 drop came from a sector-wide selloff rather than Qualcomm\u0027s own news. RSI 35 and a close below the lower Bollinger Band show the stock is heavily sold, which often leads to a short-term bounce. The next real test is the early-February 2027 results.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Apple fully exits as a modem customer and global smartphone shipments stay weak because of high memory prices",
                             "The chip unit\u0027s margin keeps falling if wafer, packaging and memory costs do not ease by the March 2027 quarter",
                             "Data-center AI200/AI250 sales or the Amazon deal ramp more slowly than promised",
                             "A sector-wide chip selloff that drags QCOM down whatever its own news",
                             "Licensing (QTL) revenue erodes as the handset market shrinks"
                         ],
               "summary":  "QCOM is in a technical downtrend. It closed at $149.94, below all three EMAs and the lower Bollinger Band, with MACD negative and volume 1.5x normal. Two things drove the drop: weak FY27 profit guidance on 2026-11-04 as Apple leaves and handsets fall 26%, and a broad chip selloff on 2026-11-20. The licensing and patent moat is intact but slowly shrinking with the handset market. The thesis is moving toward cars, PCs and data-center AI, which grew strongly but has not yet replaced the core business. Thesis status: watch."
